Lighting Control for Load Shedding — 5.4  Stock Utility Bill Impacts

for the maximum, mean, and median bill among each building's available electricity rate structures](92502_images/image_000010_2ccb8e0f491db7e75ab496e2b6936923eadf57e7563e186f33dfc48e266cb515.png)

Three horizontal box-and-whisker plots of annual utility bill cost savings across applicable ComStock buildings, one row each for With Max Bill, With Mean Bill, and With Median Bill, on an x-axis labeled Utility Bill Cost Savings [%] running from -2.0% to 6.0% with a vertical reference line at 0.0%. All three interquartile boxes sit entirely in positive territory, spanning roughly 1.3% to 2.8% with medians near 1.8% to 2.0%; whiskers run from about -1.0% to about 5.0%. The Max Bill row is shifted slightly left of the other two, which the text reads as evidence that the rate structure producing the highest bill benefits less from peak reduction or lacks a demand component. These roughly 2% median savings exceed the roughly 1% stock-level savings in Figure 9 because 32% of stock floor area is not applicable and dilutes the stock figure. The text attributes the savings to net energy reduction (dominant), time-of-use rates whose peak price hours coincide with the shed window, and monthly or seasonal demand charge reduction, and notes these understate a daily-peak measure because most rate structures charge demand monthly or seasonally.

Lighting Control for Load Shedding — 5.4  Stock Utility Bill Impacts

for the lighting load shed upgrade, five rows with model counts, on an x-axis from -100% to 100%](92502_images/image_000011_422b777e91bf9db8471784345c182b1e0cfac9d4840a29aeb657b5007605c6bd.png)

Violin plot panel titled Upgrade 25.0: light_shed (unweighted), showing the distribution of percent utility bill savings across individual applicable ComStock models, one horizontal violin with an inner box plot per fuel category. The x-axis, Percent Utility Bill Savings by Fuel (%), runs from -100 to 100 with a vertical reference line at zero. Rows top to bottom with model counts: Propane Bill (n=4614), Fuel Oil Bill (n=4697), Natural Gas Bill (n=37380), Electricity Bill w/ Mean Rate (n=80322), and Total Bill w/ Mean Electricity Rate (n=80305). All five distributions are extremely narrow and centered essentially at zero, so almost every model sees a bill change of only a few percent; the visible structure is in the scattered outlier points, which spread across the full axis in both directions for the electricity and total-bill rows and extend far to the right for natural gas. The electricity and total-bill violins sit marginally positive, matching the roughly 2% median savings in Figure 10, while the natural gas violin sits marginally negative because dimming lighting reduces internal heat gain and increases heating demand for the non-electric heating equipment present in 20% to 40% of applicable floor area.
